A||||8||mrfixit58||Off Road Fiero Idea! Help!||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||01-29-2001||05:32 PM||||I was talking to my buddy the other day. He just purchased 40 acres and a 4 wheeler. He has plans to build but, in the mean time, he has six kids (a yours and mine thing) and one 4 wheeler just won't do. I started thinking about out little car and thought, "why not strip a coupe 4 banger and turn it into a buggy". <P>Can anyone see any reason why I couldn't take a Fiero and strip it of all panels and glass, remove the doors and interior, add light weight seats, and big tires and run it around the woods? Any thoughts, suggstions, or ideas?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||TheFiero64||01-29-2001||05:56 PM||||I saw Bob Parker's son run an 85 base coupe around in the woods behind kickhill farm. It really looked like a lot of fun. He was really flying with it. It was amazing it didn't flip over. He was doing like 50. Up and down hills and all. He took a couple scared Fiero enthusiasts for a joy ride in it.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>88 Coupe V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000002||ChadMan||01-29-2001||07:42 PM||||I would think an 84 Coupe with the 4.10 gear and some tall, skinny all-season tires would go like stink offroad, for the same reason Beetles do:  a low first gear and a torquey 4-banger mounted over the drive wheels.<P>Ground clearance really isn't that bad, and who knows:  with the body panels and fender liners removed, maybe you could even get some 15" steel wheels with 31.5 tires on there!||||reg||3
Z||000003||Black88GT||01-29-2001||07:55 PM||||I have been looking for an 84 4.10 ration 4 speed for like years now.  I can't seem to find one thats beat up.  I was going to do the same thing, strip anything thats not needed for off roading and use it.  I have since given up and am looking for a Jeep.  Who knows I might get lucky and find a Fiero like that....<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||01-29-2001||08:43 PM||||When I had my first Fiero, I had taken it into some backwood trails. It went through the trails easier than any truck I had taken through them. (no mud, just sandy trails, must have been that "special rally suspension")||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||01-29-2001||09:04 PM||||Sounds like fun, but I prefer a Jeep for off-roading.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||01-29-2001||09:07 PM||||Of course, there's alos the Jalapeno kit <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/jalapeno.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/fierohq/jalapeno.html</A> <P>Seems to be a street-legal, off-road sports car... ||||reg||10
Z||000007||Standard||01-30-2001||12:48 AM||||Careful Fierosound, you're going to give the auto companies a new SUV buzzword...<P>"presenting the new Pontiac Thingamajig-tek, the first SLORSC!"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000008||mrfixit58||01-30-2001||07:39 AM||||Thanks guys,<P>Hey, it sounds like cheap fun. If I can find one for around $500 that is running, I may have to go for it. I was thinking that I may have to install a skid plate over the coolant lines and gas tank. I also thought about removing the wind shield and the back window and adding some structural reinforcment in the form of welded tubing from the roof line down to the front and back to the rear shock area. Throw in a couple of 5 point harness and off you go!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
